helpbuoy-server for Debian
====================

The server runs as the systemd service helpbuoy-server, as the system user
helpbuoy-server, with its host key, techies and registered computers in
/var/lib/helpbuoy-server.

The server runs in one of three modes, set with HELPBUOY_SERVER_MODE in
/etc/default/helpbuoy-server: ssh (the default), http or https.

In ssh mode the server serves plain SSH without WebSocket and TLS (direct
connect) on :5959; open that port in the firewall (and forward it on the
router if the server is behind NAT). Users type help.example.com:5959; the
apps try HTTPS first and then plain SSH.

Behind a reverse proxy the apps reach the server over HTTPS on port 443, and
users only type the host name. In http mode the server
listens on plain HTTP on 127.0.0.1:5959, behind a reverse proxy (Apache,
nginx or HAProxy) that serves HTTPS and passes the path /helpbuoy on as a
WebSocket. The proxy must keep the Host header, add X-Forwarded-For and allow
long idle connections. With Apache 2.4.47 or later:

  ProxyPreserveHost On
  ProxyPass /helpbuoy http://127.0.0.1:5959/helpbuoy upgrade=websocket timeout=3600

and with nginx:

  location /helpbuoy {
      proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:5959;
      proxy_http_version 1.1;
      proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
      proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
      proxy_set_header Host $host;
      proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
      proxy_read_timeout 1h;
      proxy_send_timeout 1h;
  }

HAProxy works in HTTP mode with "option forwardfor" and "timeout tunnel 1h".
Users then only type the host name, e.g. help.example.com.

Without a reverse proxy, the server can serve HTTPS itself: set
HELPBUOY_SERVER_MODE=https and HELPBUOY_SERVER_OPTS="--acme --acme-host
help.example.com" in /etc/default/helpbuoy-server (see the comments there),
give the service the capability to bind port 443 as described in the unit
file, and run "systemctl restart helpbuoy-server".

To host many helpdesks, one per host name (in http or https mode), set
HELPBUOY_SERVER_VHOSTS in /etc/default/helpbuoy-server and add them with
"sudo helpbuoy-serverctl vhost add acme.relay.example.com". The admin
commands below then take --vhost acme.relay.example.com.

The server refuses computers until it knows who runs it: set the company
name and phone number, which users waiting for a techie see, with the info
command below (or on the desk app's Server tab).

Admin commands go through helpbuoy-serverctl, which runs helpbuoy-server as the
service user on the service's state:

  sudo helpbuoy-serverctl hostkey              # fingerprint to give to techies
  sudo helpbuoy-serverctl techie add alice 'ssh-ed25519 AAAA...'
  sudo helpbuoy-serverctl techie list
  sudo helpbuoy-serverctl techie remove alice
  sudo helpbuoy-serverctl computer list
  sudo helpbuoy-serverctl computer remove SHA256:...
  sudo helpbuoy-serverctl info --company 'Example Inc' --phone '+1 234 567 890'

The running server picks up changes within a second. Logs are in the
journal (journalctl -u helpbuoy-server) and in /var/lib/helpbuoy-server/server.log.

Updates come from the HelpBuoy APT repository, which the package adds on
installation in /etc/apt/sources.list.d/helpbuoy.sources, so "apt upgrade"
installs new versions. An upgrade restarts the server, which ends running
sessions, so unattended-upgrades leaves helpbuoy-server alone; the HelpBuoy
client and desk app do update unattended. Remove the sources file to stop
updates from the repository; an upgrade does not add it again.

"apt purge helpbuoy-server" deletes /var/lib/helpbuoy-server, including the host key:
computers registered with this server would then see a changed server identity.
